  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 151 AM CDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\nAbove normal temperatures are expected to continue through the next \nweek. As the ridge axis begins to shift eastward on Friday and \nSaturday, a tightening surface pressure gradient will lead to breezy \nconditions. Fire weather concerns are low despite the prevailing dry \nconditions due to moisture advection earlier in the week. However, \nany dry advection could lead to an increase in the fire weather risk \nFriday or Saturday. \n\nBehind the ridge, a trough will lift out of the Rockies and into the \nnorthern Plains. While the axis of moisture advection will stay to \nour west in eastern Nebraska and western Iowa, increased cloud cover \nis likely through the weekend with the broad synoptic lift. \nAssociated with the trough will be a surface cold front, which will \nslowly sag through the Upper Midwest. Forcing along the front will \nlead to a low chance (10-30%) for showers Monday, which will be our \nfirst chance for measurable rainfall since October 16. As this front \nmoves across the CWA, it is expected to stall out, providing a \nfurther focus for precipitation next week.",
